"""Human adjudications backed by raw-inspection and diagnostic period scans."""
import csv,json,hashlib
from pathlib import Path
B=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
Q=list(csv.DictReader((B/'queue.csv').open(newline='')))
DEC={
'95924853':('NON_ECLIPSE_VARIABLE','Sector 39 contains four rounded brightenings around BTJD 2363–2372; two selected cores are +8.4 and +7.6 ppt. A 2.743 d diagnostic peak describes this short modulation. Sector 66 has a much larger broad excursion with data gaps, while sectors 103–104 have unstable raw scales. No detached eclipse shape or secure cross-sector period emerges.','Short-sector modulation is evident in SAP; physical class and exact source remain unresolved. Corrected-flux suppression and centroid shifts alone do not localize it.','Independent aperture and difference imaging if the modulation is pursued.'),
'155173365':('UNRESOLVED','The sector-84 selected +3.0 ppt core is a short rise and decay near BTJD 3605.25, superposed on a large sector trend and post-gap level changes. Other sectors have broad drifts, with no matching discrete positive occasion.','A flare-like positive event is present in SAP, but its source and whether it is stellar are insecure. No eclipse period is measured.','Inspect pixel-level difference image and nearby light curves for the sector-84 impulse.'),
'147473756':('NON_ECLIPSE_VARIABLE','Sector 47 selected +5.2 ppt crest is part of a repeated rounded wave. Sector 20 has multiple similar waves; sectors 60 and 74 show broad recurring modulation. Diagnostic peaks are 5.912, 2.910 (harmonic), 6.494 and 5.829 d.','Quasi-periodic non-eclipse modulation is clear; its coherence, physical class and aperture source are not established. The 34.337 d Gaia SB2 orbit is context only.','Source-localized aperture photometry and longer-baseline mode analysis.'),
'43868028':('UNRESOLVED','The sector-5 selected +4.1 ppt window lies on a monotonic rise (pre/post step +9.7 ppt), and the same curved sector-scale ramp appears in sector 32. Sector 98 has a separate sharp +20.0 ppt impulse followed by decay and an underlying drifting baseline. The 10.58–10.60 d diagnostic peaks in sectors 5/32 can reflect repeated instrumental or astrophysical shape. TESSCut s98 peak-minus-pre pixels show a positive/negative dipole around the WCS target (5.14,5.06), with +61.6 counts in the nearest-pixel-centered 3×3 but only +84 counts across the whole cutout.','The sector-5 selected window is a slope false alarm. Sector-98 has an insecure transient: its dipole is consistent with pointing or background structure, yet simple unregistered difference imaging does not exclude concurrent stellar brightening.','Registered image modeling or independent photometry of the sector-98 transient and recurring sector-scale ramp.'),
'52152553':('NON_ECLIPSE_VARIABLE','Repeated sharp-rise/slow-decay sawteeth occur throughout sectors 2, 27, 28 and 68. Diagnostic SAP peaks are 3.064, 3.050, 3.064 and 3.056 d; selected positive cores are +3.5 to +4.0 ppt. The 54.067 d Gaia spectroscopic orbit does not explain this shape or establish an eclipse.','Strong non-eclipse variability is present in the aperture; subtype and varying Gaia source remain unresolved. KSPSAP suppression and small centroid offsets are not source proof.','Difference imaging or stable alternate-aperture photometry to localize the 3.06 d signal.'),
'146516744':('REFUTED','The sector-32 +3.7 ppt selected core is driven by a few cadences around BTJD 2199.83–2199.88 at the sector endpoint. The first three positive cadences reach about +18, +15 and +10 ppt, while SAP_BKG climbs from 1688 to 2514 counts and then 9828 by BTJD 2199.931 (versus 81 counts at 2199.779). SAP_Y shifts about 0.025 pixel at onset. The TESSCut peak-minus-pre positive-pixel moment is (5.77,3.24), 2.74 pixels from the target WCS (5.61,5.98); the nearest-pixel-centered target 3×3 gains only +1.7 counts while pixels outside target 5×5 gain +94.7 counts.','The selected target brightening is refuted by off-target positive flux plus concurrent background/position disturbance. The image moment is descriptive, not a fitted PSF position. An unmodeled target flare coincident with contamination cannot be excluded absolutely, and this says nothing about weaker separate binary signals.','Only revisit with registered source-resolved pixels or an independent target-localized repeat.'),
'433693445':('NON_ECLIPSE_VARIABLE','The sector-17 +20.0 ppt impulse near BTJD 1783.64 rises abruptly over three quality-zero cadences and decays over about 0.2 d, a flare-like morphology in both SAP and KSPSAP. Local SAP_BKG fluctuates around 200–600 counts without a monotonic ramp matching the impulse; SAP_X/Y shift only a few millipixels. TESSCut peak-minus-pre positive-pixel moment is (5.80,4.99) close to target WCS (5.78,5.09), with +452.9 counts in the nearest-pixel-centered target 3×3. Eight other sectors do not show the same impulse.','The target-centered excess supports non-eclipse variability consistent with a stellar flare, but same-sector pixels cannot resolve a subpixel blend or prove flare physics. The image moment is descriptive, not a fitted PSF position. KSPSAP uses the same exposures. No orbital timing inference.','Higher resolution source photometry if the flare source matters.'),
'146169279':('UNRESOLVED','The sector-90 +3.9 ppt broad mound at BTJD 3759.39 is cut by a 1.005 d internal gap; its raw background median shifts by about 2147 counts relative to local sides. Sector 99 instead shows a segment level reset and sector 100 no corresponding event.','The gap hides part of the event, while the background change raises contamination as an alternative. Neither establishes that the pre-gap mound is instrumental. Its source and physical class remain insecure; no repeat period or eclipse interpretation is supported.','Resolve the sector-90 mound with pixel photometry or an independent well-covered recurrence.'),
'58171740':('UNRESOLVED','Sector 44 has a narrow +3.3 ppt symmetric-looking impulse around BTJD 2523.05, on a slow downward baseline. Sectors 43 and 70 show unrelated trends and no matched impulse. Both SAP and KSPSAP contain the local peak.','A real aperture excursion is possible, but its stellar origin and classification are insecure. No eclipse interpretation or period is supported.','Pixel-level source check for the sector-44 impulse.'),
'73312747':('UNRESOLVED','Sector 20 has a +3.6 ppt sharp impulse at BTJD 1862.18; sector 47 has a smaller +2.2 ppt bump at BTJD 2590.23 while its baseline rises by +5.1 ppt across the window. Other sectors show large secular trends rather than clean events.','The two spikes need not be the same phenomenon or source. Neither is an eclipse and the long separation does not yield a measured recurrence period.','Localize each impulse with image-level photometry before classifying them as stellar flares.'),
}
rows=[]
for q in Q:
 tic=q['tic']; verdict,evidence,limits,nxt=DEC[tic];dir=B/tic
 raw=json.loads((dir/'raw-inspection.json').read_text());scan=json.loads((dir/'period-scan.json').read_text())
 sectors=', '.join(f"{s['sector']} [{s['start_btjd']:.2f}, {s['end_btjd']:.2f}]" for s in raw['sectors'] if 'start_btjd' in s)
 wins='; '.join(f"s{w['sector']} BTJD {w['screen_btjd']:.3f}, {w['kind']}, core {w['core_delta_ppt']:+.1f} ppt, pre/post {w['pre_post_step_ppt']:+.1f} ppt, max gap {w['max_internal_gap_days']:.3f} d" for w in raw['windows'])
 pixel=f" For the deciding pixel check, `tesscut-receipt.json` binds the fetched cutout and `pixel-difference.json`/`.png` give the target WCS, exact frame intervals, nearest-integer-centered apertures and unregistered image difference. This is a same-sector reduction, not an independent observing occasion." if (dir/'pixel-difference.json').exists() else ''
 body=f"# {q['sample_id']} — TIC {tic}\n\nPhysical verdict: **{verdict}** for the selected brightening lead. Bounded review: **COMPLETE**. Catalogue novelty: **UNKNOWN**; four VizieR gates remain unavailable in the central catalogue check.\n\nGaia DR3 {q['gaia_dr3']} ({q['nss_type']}) has spectroscopic period {float(q['gaia_period_days']):.6f} d. Selection from Gaia SB systems does not prove a photometric binary signal. All cached sector raw SAP, QUALITY=0, was inspected: {sectors}. The screening windows are predictions, not event centers.\n\n## Deciding raw evidence\n\n{evidence}\n\nSelected-window measurements: {wins}. These local medians describe observed aperture flux, not fitted event depths. `raw-context.png` shows every full sector and every selected window; `raw-inspection.json` records cadence coverage, gaps, alternate FITS columns and source hashes. `period-scan.json` contains per-sector diagnostic 0.5–15 d Lomb–Scargle peaks after linear detrending; they are not orbital periods.{pixel}\n\n## Limits and next check\n\n{limits} No eclipse epoch or depth is promoted. Same photons in KSPSAP are a reduction check, not a new observing occasion. {nxt}\n\n## Provenance\n\nThe frozen packet at `../../source-packets/{tic}/sources.json` binds the Gaia metadata, FITS identities and original bulk/fold receipts. `../SOURCE-HASHES.json` rechecks them; `../SHA256SUMS.json` seals this review.\n"
 (dir/'REPORT.md').write_text(body,encoding='utf8')
 rows.append({'sample_id':q['sample_id'],'tic':tic,'sample_stratum':q['sample_stratum'],'physical_verdict':verdict,'full_review_completion':'COMPLETE','novelty_status':'UNKNOWN','period_days':'','period_error_days':'','primary_epoch_btjd':'','depth_ppt':'','limitations':limits,'next_check':nxt,'report':str((dir/'REPORT.md').resolve())})
with (B/'RESULTS.csv').open('w',newline='',encoding='utf8') as f:
 w=csv.DictWriter(f,fieldnames=list(rows[0]));w.writeheader();w.writerows(rows)
print({v:sum(r['physical_verdict']==v for r in rows) for v in sorted({r['physical_verdict'] for r in rows})})
